00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:51.258
- Yep.

00:00:51.258 --> 00:00:52.190
- And you went with your son?

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:53.338
- Yes.

00:00:53.338 --> 00:00:54.330
- So was that cool to do?

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.296
Was it cool for him to
see like how you grew up.

00:00:56.296 --> 00:00:57.910
- You know, the weird thing is my family

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:00.580
has a reality show called
Alaska the Last Frontier.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:01.900
And so he watches that

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:03.689
and so he's gotten a
taste of it virtually.

00:01:03.689 --> 00:01:04.580
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:06.190
- [Jewel] But always like
when I get to bring him home

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:08.632
and make him haul the water
and chop the wood it's good

00:01:08.632 --> 00:01:09.970
- I was like it's different
from like watching it

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:11.030
and actually having to do it.

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:11.960
- Yeah, totally.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:13.270
- It's a totally different life.

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:15.840
And even watching that,
just, I keep getting reminded

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:16.760
of your upbringing.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:17.780
It's crazy cool.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:20.470
It's like straight up out of
the shows they're making now

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:21.900
like "Yellowstone", almost.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:22.733
- [Jewel] Exactly.

00:01:22.733 --> 00:01:23.890
- Yeah. I feel like it's
like how they're trying

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:24.723
to make films now.

00:01:24.723 --> 00:01:25.556
It's like, yeah, I did that.

00:01:25.556 --> 00:01:26.520
That's how I grew up.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.110
So you recently opened up
about having panic attacks.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:30.150
I didn't know that about you.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:31.810
- Yeah. I moved out at 15

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:33.890
so I started having panic
attacks about that age.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:35.080
Didn't know what they were.

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:38.650
And then when I was homeless,
they hit a whole new level.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:40.300
And so I started inventing tools

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.840
and techniques to help me with them.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:43.360
Haven't had them in ages.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:44.859
And then suddenly when I was filming,

00:01:44.859 --> 00:01:46.320
maybe it was "The Masked Singer",

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.050
I had my first panic attack in ages.

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:49.410
- Well, probably because you were

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:51.080
in a costume that you couldn't breath in.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.340
- Claustrophobic. I was
a little claustrophobic.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:54.430
Yeah. So I started posting

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:57.160
because we helped kids
with anxiety disorders.

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:58.280
Those types of things.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:01:59.350
And so I started posting more

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.200
about it just to help people
understand what they are

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.530
and the things you can do to help.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:04.807
- I had no idea what they
were until I was pregnant.

00:02:04.807 --> 00:02:07.590
The first time I was pregnant
and I had one while driving

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:08.423
- Oh God, that's the worst.

00:02:08.423 --> 00:02:09.256
- And I was terrified.

00:02:09.256 --> 00:02:10.180
It was like, I froze.

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:12.920
And I'd never had one
before, but for some reason

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:14.710
I don't know chemically,
something happened

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:17.090
and I had one, I was
like, this is no good.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:17.923
Like you freak out.

00:02:17.923 --> 00:02:19.414
- Your brain literally goes offline.

00:02:19.414 --> 00:02:21.870
So all the blood drains
outta your frontal lobes

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:23.400
and goes right to your amygdala

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:24.810
and your vascular system changes.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.010
Your blood pressure changes.

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:26.843
And so

00:02:26.843 --> 00:02:28.097
- What's up Bill Nye?

00:02:28.097 --> 00:02:29.050
- Finding things to go.

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:31.160
- Geez. I was like, OK, that's amazing.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:33.751
- Well, if you find things
that force the, the blood back

00:02:33.751 --> 00:02:38.040
into your processing centers,
so smell, taste, touch.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:40.050
That forces your brain to
deal with the information.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:42.015
So it forces it to go back online.

00:02:42.015 --> 00:02:43.324
- To come back to the scene a little.

00:02:43.324 --> 00:02:44.157
- Oh yeah.

00:02:44.157 --> 00:02:45.072
- What in the world?

00:02:45.072 --> 00:02:46.632
Okay. Have you guys ever suffered

00:02:46.632 --> 00:02:48.410
from any kind of panic attack

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:50.870
or any kind of maybe
like on set or something?

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:51.703
Do you ever get like that with?

00:02:51.703 --> 00:02:53.900
Like I would get terrified
of what y'all do.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.260
- But I think it's so validating

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.270
hearing the kind of scientific side of it

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:59.611
To hear that it is a
chemical reaction happening

00:02:59.611 --> 00:03:00.444
and something that needs to be addressed

00:03:00.444 --> 00:03:01.277
in that way.

00:03:01.277 --> 00:03:02.540
- [Jewel] And it's not rational.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:04.160
- Yeah. That reasoning part of your brain

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:05.140
literally goes offline.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.210
There's no blood there.

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:07.150
Yeah. It's fascinating.

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:08.330
- And that's the panic.

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:09.163
- [Jewel] Yeah.

00:03:09.163 --> 00:03:11.200
Yeah. Have you ever
had anything like that?

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:12.720
- Of course. Yeah, absolutely.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.253
That's just something that just puts you

00:03:14.253 --> 00:03:16.676
in your place completely
and you can't move. Yeah.

00:03:16.676 --> 00:03:19.260
- Yeah. And it's your
brain noticing patterns.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:21.498
It only takes two data
points that your brain

00:03:21.498 --> 00:03:24.350
even your subconscious recognizes

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:27.540
to some other time that might
have been traumatic for you.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:28.700
And so it's, I don't always, you know

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:29.800
it took me a long time to figure out like

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.860
what were those two things, you know?

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:32.693
- [Kelly] Oh, there's a whole
how the body keeps the score.

00:03:32.693 --> 00:03:34.250
So then I could start creating a plan.

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:35.083
- Yeah.

00:03:35.083 --> 00:03:36.220
- Cause you go back to that
trauma and you don't realize

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:37.230
you're doing it, but your brain does that.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:38.580
- But your brain sees the pattern.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:39.740
Yeah, it's fascinating.

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:40.899
- [Kelly] It's exhausting.

00:03:40.899 --> 00:03:43.416
So your new album is
called "Freewheelin' Woman".

00:03:43.416 --> 00:03:45.052
So why did you choose that title?

00:03:45.052 --> 00:03:46.580
I love it.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:47.730
- I, you know, I'm just really proud

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:50.150
that I've been able to have my own life.

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:51.120
You know, it's hard for women.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:52.030
I think it's really hard.

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:53.730
And I've been able to make
a living with my words

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:54.800
with my songs.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:55.633
- [Kelly] But also have a life.

00:03:55.633 --> 00:03:58.130
- And have the autonomy
to move around the world

00:03:58.130 --> 00:03:59.230
and my careers, as I saw fit.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:00.679
And you know, this job
is not kind to women.

00:04:00.679 --> 00:04:01.568
It's hard on women.

00:04:01.568 --> 00:04:02.950
It's hard on women as we age.

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:04.039
And I'm just proud.

00:04:04.039 --> 00:04:05.490
I'm proud of my age.

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:06.940
I think I'm singing
better than I ever sung.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:08.059
I think I'm writing better
than I ever written.

00:04:08.059 --> 00:04:10.020
- My voice is better than when
I was younger, I feel like.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:13.075
- Yeah. So I Feel like
I just wanted the album

00:04:13.075 --> 00:04:14.960
to represent just a sense of empowerment.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:15.793
- Yeah.

00:04:15.793 --> 00:04:16.626
- I love this age.

00:04:16.626 --> 00:04:17.459
I love where I'm at

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:19.510
and I don't wanna have to
pretend something else, you know

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:20.519
- [Kelly] It's exhausting.

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:21.352
- Yeah.

00:04:21.352 --> 00:04:22.870
- [Kelly] I'm not into
things that tire me.

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:23.703
You will be singing.

00:04:23.703 --> 00:04:24.784
It's called "Long Way 'Round", right?

00:04:24.784 --> 00:04:25.617
- [Jewel] Yeah.

00:04:25.617 --> 00:04:26.450
- "Long Way 'Round".

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:27.283
Oh my God.

00:04:27.283 --> 00:04:28.116
It's so good.

00:04:28.116 --> 00:04:30.560
And in this song I wanted to
talk about, because this is

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:31.940
one of the lines and I love it.

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:33.481
"It's sometimes it's the long way round.

00:04:33.481 --> 00:04:36.210
You gotta get lost to get found."

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:37.160
I love that.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:38.470
So what, what about that?

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:40.120
Like why did you write that lyric?

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:42.060
- My life has definitely
been the long way around.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.950
It's been a lot of hard plot
twists that haven't been fun.

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:48.040
That haven't been easy,
but they made me who I am

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:49.072
and I do feel good.

00:04:49.072 --> 00:04:51.730
Really I feel so happy
where I'm at in my life.

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:52.959
- I love that for you.

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:53.792
- And so just that encouragement of,

00:04:53.792 --> 00:04:54.870
sometimes it is the long way around.

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:55.703
Sometimes it's messy.

00:04:55.703 --> 00:04:57.765
Life is so messy but you
come out the other side.

00:04:57.765 --> 00:04:58.598
And I wanted that so to be encouraging.

00:04:58.598 --> 00:04:59.980
- If there's no messy part though

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:01.957
I don't feel like you
really are truly living.

00:05:01.957 --> 00:05:02.980
Like, you know what I'm saying?

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:04.700
You gotta kind of get in
there to figure it out.

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:05.630
- [Jewel] You do, you
got to break a few eggs.

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:06.530
- Yeah, yeah.

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:07.943
- Yep.

00:05:07.943 --> 00:05:10.547
- [Kelly] Ugh, eggs. So
broken some of those.

00:05:10.547 --> 00:05:11.380
(Jewel is laughing)

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:12.213
- Well stick around

00:05:12.213 --> 00:05:13.680
because Jewel is performing for us later.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:14.540
I heard the rehearsal.

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:15.870
You do not want to miss this,

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:18.170
her new album, "Freewheelin'
Woman", is out now.

